From: George Watson <george@hellas.ai>
Date: Tue, 12 May 2026 14:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] server: skip kvm-vision init entirely when HDMI is disabled

`initialize()` always calls `common.GetKvmVision()`, which through cgo
fires the vendor SDK's `kvmv_init` -> `SAMPLE_COMM_VI_ParseIni` ->
sensor-config probe. On mainline kernels the cv1800b camera/IPS
sample helpers can't find the vendor sensor wiring, ParseIni returns
0xffffffff, and the helper then dereferences a null sensor descriptor
and SEGVs. The whole nanokvm-server crash-loops at startup with
`Main process exited, code=killed, status=11/SEGV`.

The existing `utils.IsHdmiDisabled()` check is honored further down,
but only to skip `SetHDMI(true)` -- it doesn't guard `GetKvmVision()`,
which is what actually runs the SDK init. Move the whole vision
init under the same guard so that when `/etc/kvm/hdmi_disabled`
exists, the server boots without ever touching the camera subsystem.

This lets the web UI come up on platforms where mainline doesn't (yet)
have working HDMI capture, while still preserving the original code
path on the vendor 5.10 image-based-appliance kernel.
---
diff -u a/server/main.go b/server/main.go
--- a/server/main.go
+++ b/server/main.go
@@ -37,11 +37,17 @@
 	// init screen parameters
 	_ = common.GetScreen()

-	// init HDMI
-	vision := common.GetKvmVision()
-	vision.SetHDMI(false)
-	time.Sleep(10 * time.Millisecond)
+	// init HDMI. Guarded behind IsHdmiDisabled() so that on platforms
+	// where the vendor `kvmv_init` cgo binding SEGVs (mainline kernels
+	// without the cv1800 sample VI / sensor INI), creating
+	// /etc/kvm/hdmi_disabled stops the server from crash-looping. The
+	// original code always called GetKvmVision() — which is the
+	// segfaulting path — and only used IsHdmiDisabled() to decide
+	// whether to re-enable after the initial toggle.
 	if !utils.IsHdmiDisabled() {
+		vision := common.GetKvmVision()
+		vision.SetHDMI(false)
+		time.Sleep(10 * time.Millisecond)
 		vision.SetHDMI(true)
 	}

